meta {
  name: Callback
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

get {
  url: {{baseURL}}/google-calendar/callback/
  body: none
  auth: none
}

params:query {
  ~code: {{google_oauth_code}}
  ~state: {{google_oauth_state}}
}

headers {
  Accept: application/json
}

settings {
  encodeUrl: true
}

docs {
  Google redirects the staff member's browser here after consent - not
  normally called directly, since the real flow is one browser round trip
  (Connect.bru/PersonalConnect.bru's auth_url -> Google's consent screen ->
  here -> ADMIN_FRONTEND_URL). This request lets you replay that last hop by
  hand.

  Unlike an earlier version of this flow, there is no separate /complete/
  step anymore: this one view unsigns `state` (google_oauth.unsign_oauth_state)
  to learn which flow started it (and, for the personal flow, which Biopeak
  user - see PersonalConnect.bru), exchanges `code` with Google for real
  tokens, fetches the connected account's email, and saves everything itself
  - all before redirecting. It redirects to ADMIN_FRONTEND_URL with a plain
  outcome flag: `#gcal_connected=1` on success, or `#gcal_error=<message>`
  on any failure (denied consent, expired/tampered state, wrong account for
  the shared flow, etc.) - never the raw code, never the tokens.

  To test this directly rather than through a real browser round trip:
  `state` must be a value this same server actually signed - copy it out of
  Connect.bru's or PersonalConnect.bru's `auth_url` response (the `state=`
  query param in that URL) within 10 minutes, and pair it with a fresh
  `code` copied out of the browser's address bar right after approving
  consent (codes are one-time-use, so a stale one only exercises the
  "failed to exchange with Google" error path, not the happy path).

  Method: GET
  Path: /google-calendar/callback/
  Source: integrations.google_calendar.views.GoogleCalendarCallbackView
  Auth: None (public) - Google's redirect can't carry our JWT, which is
  exactly why the personal flow's user identity has to ride inside the
  signed `state` instead of request.user.
}
